{
  "dom_id": "step-q344341",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059223/assets/28cef739fd66e414894e830a02582f979dfdf285b3208cff5bba42b68537eefa.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ at the point $x=8$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=6$ and $x=10.$*\n\n${f}^{\u2032}(8)\u2248$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)\u2248\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=8$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(8) & \u2248\\frac{f(8+2)-f(8-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)-f(6)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)}{4} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
